--TEST--
`Core\Request::header` answers the same way for every field name it is given when no request arrived, so nothing about the name reaches the decision -- `rule:security/request-state-throws-in-an-isolate`, spec § 15
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Invariance over a sweep, counted rather than read off a line: `header` is one
// of the two readers of what arrived with the request line, and the one of them
// that takes an argument -- so it is the one that could answer differently for
// one field than for another. It must not. Whether a request arrived is decided
// before the name is looked at, and a member that answered `null` for a field
// nobody sent would be telling a caller that a request it never received
// carried no such header.
uint $refused = 0;
try {
    Core\Request::header("content-type");
} catch (LogicError $ordinary) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}
try {
    Core\Request::header("Content-Type");
} catch (LogicError $capitalised) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}
try {
    Core\Request::header("");
} catch (LogicError $empty) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}
try {
    Core\Request::header("x-forwarded-for");
} catch (LogicError $repeatable) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}
try {
    Core\Request::header("a name: with a colon and spaces in it");
} catch (LogicError $unspellable) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}
echo "every name refused: ", $refused, " of 5\n";

// The case-insensitive match is a reading of a request that arrived, so the two
// spellings above are not two answers here -- there is nothing to match
// against, and both stop one step earlier.
uint $plural = 0;
try {
    Core\Request::headers();
} catch (LogicError $all) {
    $plural = $plural + 1;
}
echo "its no-argument sibling refused: ", $plural, " of 1\n";
--EXPECT--
every name refused: 5 of 5
its no-argument sibling refused: 1 of 1
